<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cîteaux Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1098">1098</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cîteaux Abbey, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, was founded in 1098 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-of-molesme">Robert of Molesme</a> and a group of monks seeking a stricter observance of the Rule of Saint Benedict. It became the mother house of the Cistercian order, which spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a>. Its monks built up the walled vineyard of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clos-de-vougeot">Clos de Vougeot</a>. Dissolved in the French Revolution, it was resettled by monks in 1898.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/citeaux-abbey.jpg" alt="Cîteaux Abbey" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: G CHP · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abbaye_de_C%C3%AEteaux_La_Biblioth%C3%A8que.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cîteaux Abbey stands in Saint-Nicolas-lès-Cîteaux, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a>. When the monks arrived the site was wooded and swampy, in a sparsely populated area. The place name is older than the abbey and its origin is uncertain. One theory derives it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> cis tertium, this side of the third milestone, on the Roman road linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a> and Chalons sur <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a>; another from cisternae, which in Middle Latin could mean the stagnant pools of a swamp.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The abbey was founded on 21 March 1098, Saint Benedict's Day, by monks of Molesme Abbey led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-of-molesme">Robert of Molesme</a>, its first abbot. Renaud, Vicomte de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaune">Beaune</a>, and Odo I, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, supported it. The second abbot was Alberic and the third Stephen Harding, who wrote the Carta Caritatis describing the organisation of the order.</p><p>In 1111 the monastery produced the illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> known as the Cîteaux Moralia in Job. Bernard of Clairvaux, a monk of Cîteaux, was sent in 1115 to found Clairvaux Abbey and became its first abbot. His influence helped the order grow rapidly. By the early 13th century it had more than 500 houses.</p><p>The great church, begun about 1140 and finished in 1193, became the burial place of the Dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. As head of the order the abbey had to house the delegates of the annual chapter and the ducal family, which shaped its buildings. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> it was pillaged in 1360, 1365, 1434 and 1438. Soldiers looted it in 1589, reportedly carrying off three hundred wagonloads of booty, and monastic life was not firmly re-established until about 1610.</p><p>In 1790, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, the monks were offered pensions to return to civilian life. Fourteen refused and twenty-nine accepted. The government seized and sold the abbey, and the local population looted what remained. From 1846 an agricultural penitentiary colony for minors, run by Father Joseph Rey, occupied the site, and an 1888 scandal over its conditions was raised in the National Assembly. Trappist monks, the first four from Sept-Fons arriving on 2 October 1898, resumed monastic life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cîteaux belongs to the Trappists, also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cistercians">Cistercians</a> of the Strict Observance, and has about 35 monks. The community makes a cheese under the abbey's name, as well as caramels and honey-based candies. Three old buildings survive: the library of 1509, the Définitoire and the Lenoir building of 1771, classified as historic monuments by decree of 28 December 1978. The ninth centenary of the founding in 1998 was marked by the renovation of the church.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cîteaux Abbey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its vineyards on the slopes of the Côte d’Or, between Dijon and Beaune, were already prized in the Middle Ages, and monasteries did much to develop them: the monks of <b>Cîteaux</b> built up the walled vineyard of the Clos de Vougeot from the twelfth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
